<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>1.3. The operator ``del''</title>
  
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>


<definition xml:id="def-divergence">
  <statement>
    <p>Let <m>\mbf F(x,y,z) = (F_1, F_2, F_3)</m>. 
      The
  <term>divergence</term> of <m>\mbf F</m> is given by
  <md> \displaystyle
  \nabla\cdot\mbf F  =
  \left(
    \frac{\partial}{\partial x},
    \frac{\partial}{\partial y},
    \frac{\partial}{\partial z}
  \right)\cdot(F_1, F_2, F_3)  =
  \frac{\partial F_1}{\partial x} +
  \frac{\partial F_2}{\partial y} +
  \frac{\partial F_3}{\partial z}. 
  </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>


<definition xml:id="def-curl">
  <statement>
    <p>Let <m>\mbf F(x,y,z) = (F_1, F_2, F_3)</m>. 
      The <term>curl</term> of <m>\mbf F</m> is
      <md> \displaystyle
      \nabla\times\mbf F =
      \left|
        \begin{array}{ccc}
          \vec{i} \amp  \vec{j} \amp  \vec{k} \\
          \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
                  \amp 
                    \frac{\partial}{\partial y}
                            \amp 
                              \frac{\partial}{\partial z}      \\
          F_1 \amp  F_2 \amp  F_3 \\
        \end{array}
      \right| =
      \left(
        \pd{F_3}{y}-\pd{F_2}{z}, 
        \pd{F_1}{z}-\pd{F_3}{x},
        \pd{F_2}{x}-\pd{F_1}{y}
      \right).
      </md>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>\displaystyle \mbf F = (xy+yz, 3x+2yz, 2xy + z^2).</m>  Find the
         divergence <m>\nabla\cdot \mbf F</m>, and notice that the divergence is a
         function, rather than a field.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>
  Find <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> where <m>\mbf F = (xy+yz, 3x+2yz, 2xy +
  z^2)</m>, and notice that the result in this case is a new field.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>The divergence <m>\nabla\cdot\mbf F</m> can be defined for fields in
  any dimension, but the curl <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> only works in
  dimension three.  However, if we have a two-dimensional field <m>\mbf
  F(x,y)=(F_1(x,y), F_2(x,y))</m>, we can view it as a three-dimensional
  field by setting the third component to 0:  <m>\mbf F = (F_1, F_2,
  0)</m>.  Then we have
  <md> \displaystyle
  \nabla \times \mbf F = \left(0,0,\pd{F_2}{x}-\pd{F_1}{y}\right),
  </md>
  which we often just think of as the function
  <md>\nabla \times \mbf F = \pd{F_2}{x}-\pd{F_1}{y}.</md></p>
      <p>Find <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> if <m>\mbf F(x,y) = (-y, x)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <introduction>
      <p>Just as we found second derivatives in Calculus I, we can
  differentiate twice using <m>\nabla</m> provided that we interpret
  <m>\nabla</m> appropriately.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Start with the function <m>f(x,y,z)</m> and write the gradient <m>\nabla
  f</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Then find the curl of the gradient:  <m>\nabla\times\nabla f</m>.  (It's
  kind of a gross computation, but just be patient!)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this say about the curl <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> of a
  conservative field?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Now suppose that <m>\mbf F=(F_1, F_2, F_3)</m> and find the
  divergence of the curl: <m>\nabla\cdot(\nabla\times\mbf F)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2.5in">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        There is a very important operation we will use often, called the <em>Laplacian</em>. Let's explore.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>f(x,y,z)</m> is a function, find an expression for the
  divergence of the gradient: <m>\nabla\cdot\nabla f</m>.</p>
        <p>We call this the <em>Laplacian</em> of <m>f</m> and denote it as
  <m>\nabla^2f = \nabla\cdot\nabla f</m>.  It will play an important role
  later in the course.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Find <m>\displaystyle \nabla^2 \ln(x^2+y^2)</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that <m>\ds F(x,y) =
  \left(
    -\frac{y}{x^2+y^2},
    \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}
  \right)</m>.
  Find <m>\nabla\times\mbf F</m> and <m>\nabla \cdot \mbf F</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
